text:
Interactive

The chase
Pick a matchup and step through one simulated game from the book’s starting position (predator red, prey blue). Each agent follows its policy; moves slip to a neighboring direction 30% of the time.

chaseData = FileAttachment("data/ch25-chase.json").json()
viewof chaseName = Inputs.select(Object.keys(chaseData.runs), {label: "Matchup"})
viewof chaseT = Inputs.range([0, 59], {step: 1, value: 0, label: "Step"})























chaseNow = chaseData.runs[chaseName][chaseT]
md`Step **${chaseT}**: ${chaseNow.pred === chaseNow.prey ? "**caught!** " : ""}captures so far: **${chaseNow.caught}**.`
















{
  const S = 46, pad = 30, h = S * 0.577;
  const X = t =&gt; pad + (chaseData.tiles[t][0] + 0.5) * S, Y = t =&gt; pad + 10 - chaseData.tiles[t][1] * S;
  const hex = t =&gt; d3.range(6).map(k =&gt; [X(t) + h * Math.cos(Math.PI / 6 + k * Math.PI / 3),
                                         Y(t) + h * Math.sin(Math.PI / 6 + k * Math.PI / 3)].join(",")).join(" ");
  const tiles = chaseData.tiles.map((_, t) =&gt; svg`&lt;polygon points="${hex(t)}" fill="${chaseData.colors.tile}" stroke="#fcfcfb" stroke-width="3"/&gt;`);
  const same = chaseNow.pred === chaseNow.prey;
  return svg`&lt;svg width="${2 * pad + 6.5 * S}" height="${2 * pad + 3 * S}" style="background:#fcfcfb"&gt;${tiles}
    &lt;circle cx="${X(chaseNow.prey) + (same ? 7 : 0)}" cy="${Y(chaseNow.prey)}" r="9" fill="${chaseData.colors.prey}"/&gt;
    &lt;circle cx="${X(chaseNow.pred) - (same ? 7 : 0)}" cy="${Y(chaseNow.pred)}" r="9" fill="${chaseData.colors.pred}"/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;`;
}








Figure 2: One simulated chase on the 12-tile map of Appendix F.13: the predator (red) and the prey (blue) after the chosen number of steps.

Interactive

Nodes as belief regions
Choose a controller and step through a simulated run. The chart shows the belief a Chapter 19 filter would hold (black), which the controller never computes. The colored band shows the node the controller is in. Each node ends up standing for a range of beliefs.

runData = FileAttachment("data/ch23-runs.json").json()
viewof runName = Inputs.select(Object.keys(runData).sort(), {label: "Controller", value: "fork: count the answers (5 nodes)"})
viewof runT = Inputs.range([1, 60], {step: 1, value: 12, label: "Step"})























runNow = {
  const R = runData[runName], i = runT - 1;
  const pts = R.t.slice(0, runT).map((t, k) =&gt; ({t, b: R.b[k], node: R.nodes[R.x[k] - 1], a: R.a[k], o: R.o[k], s: R.s[k]}));
  const total = R.r.slice(0, runT).reduce((acc, r) =&gt; acc + r, 0);
  return {R, pts, now: pts[i], total};
}
md`Step **${runNow.now.t}**: node **${runNow.now.node}**, action **${runNow.now.a}**, observation **${runNow.now.o}**; true state **${runNow.now.s}**; the filter's belief ${runNow.R.ylabel} = **${runNow.now.b.toFixed(3)}**. Undiscounted reward so far: **${runNow.total}**.`
















{
  const colors = ["#2a78d6", "#eb6834", "#1baf7a", "#4a3aa7", "#e87ba4"];
  return Plot.plot({width: 640, height: 300, style: {background: "#fcfcfb"},
    x: {domain: [0.5, 60.5], label: "step"}, y: {domain: [-0.12, 1], label: runNow.R.ylabel, grid: true},
    color: {domain: runNow.R.nodes, range: colors.slice(0, runNow.R.nodes.length), legend: true},
    marks: [
      Plot.rect(runNow.pts, {x1: d =&gt; d.t - 0.5, x2: d =&gt; d.t + 0.5, y1: -0.12, y2: -0.03, fill: "node"}),
      Plot.line(runNow.pts, {x: "t", y: "b", stroke: "#0b0b0b", strokeWidth: 2, curve: "step-after"}),
      Plot.dot([runNow.now], {x: "t", y: "b", r: 5, fill: "#eb6834"})
    ]});
}








Figure 2: One simulated run of the chosen controller: the belief a filter would hold (black line) and the controller’s node at each step (colored band).




Over 20,000 steps, the two-node rule was in node I at beliefs \(P(\text{hungry})\) from 0.0 to 0.032, and in node F from 0.471 to 0.541. At every step, the rule took the action the exact policy takes at the filter’s belief, which is why it is optimal. For the counter, each node always had the same belief: \(P(\text{home right}) =\) 0.5 in the even asking node, 0.85 and 0.15 one answer ahead, and 0.97 and 0.03 when going. The belief depends only on the answers heard since the hike began, and that’s exactly what the nodes count.

Interactive

Bounds explorer
Choose a method and the number of iterations. The crying baby’s exact value is black; upper bounds should stay above it, lower bounds below.

boundsData = FileAttachment("data/ch21-bounds.json").json()
viewof boundMethod = Inputs.select(Object.keys(boundsData.series).sort(), {label: "Method", value: "QMDP (upper)"})
viewof boundIter = Inputs.select(Object.keys(boundsData.series[boundMethod]).map(Number).sort((a, b) =&gt; a - b),
                                 {label: "Iterations (or m)"})























boundNow = {
  const y = boundsData.series[boundMethod][String(boundIter)];
  const diff = y.map((v, i) =&gt; v - boundsData.exact[i]);
  return {pts: boundsData.x.map((x, i) =&gt; ({x, bound: y[i], exact: boundsData.exact[i]})),
          worst: d3.max(diff.map(Math.abs)), above: d3.min(diff) &gt;= -1e-6, below: d3.max(diff) &lt;= 1e-6};
}
md`Largest distance from the exact value: **${boundNow.worst.toFixed(3)}**. ${boundNow.above ? "The curve is on or above the exact value everywhere." : boundNow.below ? "The curve is on or below the exact value everywhere." : "The curve crosses the exact value (not yet a bound of either kind)."}`
















Plot.plot({
  width: 600, height: 330, style: {background: "#fcfcfb"},
  x: {domain: [0, 1], label: "P(hungry)"}, y: {label: "value", grid: true},
  marks: [
    Plot.line(boundNow.pts, {x: "x", y: "exact", stroke: "#0b0b0b", strokeWidth: 2.5}),
    Plot.line(boundNow.pts, {x: "x", y: "bound", stroke: "#2a78d6", strokeWidth: 2})
  ]
})








Figure 2: The chosen bound (blue) and the exact value (black) for the crying baby, over P(hungry).

Interactive

Step through the tram’s filter
Move through time and switch between the belief after predicting and after observing. Watch the predict step blur the belief and shift it forward, and the observation sharpen it again. Gray columns are tunnel stops; the orange triangle is where the tram really is.

tram = FileAttachment("data/ch19-tram.json").json()
viewof t = Inputs.range([0, tram.steps.length - 1], {step: 1, value: 0, label: "Time step t"})
viewof phase = Inputs.radio(["after predicting", "after observing"], {value: "after observing", label: "Show belief"})























md`**Step ${t}.** ${t === 0 ? "No move yet; the prior is uniform." : "The tram drove one step."} The sensor read **${tram.steps[t].dark ? "dark" : "light"}**. The tram is really at stop ${tram.steps[t].stop}.`







{
  const step = tram.steps[t];
  const b = phase === "after predicting" ? step.pred : step.post;
  const bars = b.map((p, i) =&gt; ({stop: i + 1, p}));      // stops are numbered from 1
  // Marks, back to front: full-height gray bars behind the tunnel stops, the belief bars,
  // and a downward triangle (a triangle rotated 180°) above the true stop.
  return Plot.plot({
    width: 640, height: 300, marginBottom: 40,
    style: {background: tram.style.surface, color: tram.style.ink},
    x: {domain: d3.range(1, tram.n + 1), label: "Stop", padding: 0.15},
    y: {domain: [0, 1], label: "Belief b(s)", grid: true},
    marks: [
      Plot.barY(tram.tunnels, {x: d =&gt; d, y: 1, fill: tram.style.tunnel, insetTop: 0}),
      Plot.barY(bars, {x: "stop", y: "p", fill: tram.style.blue, rx: 3, tip: true}),
      Plot.dot([step.stop], {x: d =&gt; d, y: 0.95, symbol: "triangle", r: 7, fill: tram.style.orange,
                             rotate: 180}),
      Plot.ruleY([0])
    ]
  });
}








Figure 3: The discrete filter on the tram, one step at a time. Bars show the belief over the 12 stops.






How much should you trust the sensor?
This runs the Kalman filter on 25 steps of the kayak, with the GPS readings fixed. You choose the variances the filter assumes. With a small assumed GPS noise \(\Sigma_o\), the estimate chases every reading. With a small assumed process noise \(\Sigma_s\), it trusts its own prediction and smooths the readings away. The data were generated with \(\Sigma_s = 1\) and \(\Sigma_o = 4\).

kayak = FileAttachment("data/ch19-kayak.json").json()
viewof logQ = Inputs.range([-2, 2], {step: 0.1, value: 0, label: "log₁₀ Σs (process noise)"})
viewof logR = Inputs.range([-2, 2], {step: 0.1, value: Math.log10(4), label: "log₁₀ Σo (GPS noise)"})























kf = {
  // The 1D Kalman filter, re-run from scratch whenever a slider moves. With every
  // matrix equal to 1, the update is just scalar arithmetic.
  const q = 10 ** logQ, r = 10 ** logR;        // sliders are on a log scale
  let mu = kayak.mu0, v = kayak.var0;
  return kayak.obs.map((o, i) =&gt; {
    const mp = mu + kayak.a, vp = v + q;       // predict: move 2 m, variance grows by Σs
    const K = vp / (vp + r);                   // gain: trust in the reading vs the prediction
    mu = mp + K * (o - mp); v = (1 - K) * vp;  // correct
    return {t: i + 1, mu, lo: mu - 2 * Math.sqrt(v), hi: mu + 2 * Math.sqrt(v), K};   // ±2σ band
  });
}







md`Assumed Σs = ${(10 ** logQ).toFixed(2)}, Σo = ${(10 ** logR).toFixed(2)}. The gain settles at **K = ${kf[kf.length - 1].K.toFixed(2)}**: each GPS reading moves the estimate ${Math.round(100 * kf[kf.length - 1].K)}% of the way from the prediction toward the reading.`







// Each series gets a constant "name" (e.g. stroke: () =&gt; "True position"), which the
// color scale maps to a color; that's how the legend is built.
Plot.plot({
  width: 640, height: 320,
  style: {background: kayak.style.surface, color: kayak.style.ink},
  x: {label: "Time step t"}, y: {label: "Position (m)", grid: true},
  color: {legend: true, domain: ["True position", "GPS reading", "Kalman estimate"],
          range: [kayak.style.ink, kayak.style.muted, kayak.style.blue]},
  marks: [
    Plot.areaY(kf, {x: "t", y1: "lo", y2: "hi", fill: kayak.style.blue, fillOpacity: 0.18}),
    Plot.line(kayak.truth, {x: (d, i) =&gt; i + 1, y: d =&gt; d, stroke: () =&gt; "True position", strokeWidth: 2}),
    Plot.dot(kayak.obs, {x: (d, i) =&gt; i + 1, y: d =&gt; d, fill: () =&gt; "GPS reading", r: 3}),
    Plot.line(kf, {x: "t", y: "mu", stroke: () =&gt; "Kalman estimate", strokeWidth: 2})
  ]
})








Figure 4: Kalman filter on the kayak with adjustable assumed noise. The band is the filter’s ±2σ uncertainty.

Interactive

Q-learning versus Sarsa on the towpath
Scrub through training. Each cell shows its current greedy action, and is shaded by its estimated value (darker is worse). The blue line is the greedy path from start (S) to finish (F); the bottom row between them is the canal.

towData = FileAttachment("data/ch17-towpath.json").json()
viewof towEp = Inputs.range([5, 500], {step: 5, value: 50, label: "Episodes"})
















{
  const S = 44, pad = 10, arrow = ["↑", "→", "↓", "←"];
  const panel = name =&gt; {
    const snap = towData.snaps[name][towEp / towData.every - 1];
    // Follow the greedy actions from the start (at most 40 moves) to draw the path.
    const move = [[-1, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0], [0, -1]];
    let r = towData.rows - 1, c = 0, path = [[r, c]];
    for (let k = 0; k &lt; 40 && !(r === towData.rows - 1 && c === towData.cols - 1); k++) {
      const a = snap.best[r * towData.cols + c];
      let r2 = Math.min(Math.max(r + move[a][0], 0), towData.rows - 1), c2 = Math.min(Math.max(c + move[a][1], 0), towData.cols - 1);
      if (r2 === towData.rows - 1 && c2 &gt; 0 && c2 &lt; towData.cols - 1) { r2 = towData.rows - 1; c2 = 0; }  // into the canal
      r = r2; c = c2; path.push([r, c]);
    }
    const shade = d3.scaleLinear([-30, 0], ["#9a9890", "#ffffff"]).clamp(true);
    const cells = d3.range(towData.rows * towData.cols).map(i =&gt; {
      const r = Math.floor(i / towData.cols), c = i % towData.cols;
      const canal = r === towData.rows - 1 && c &gt; 0 && c &lt; towData.cols - 1;
      const label = i === towData.start ? "S" : i === towData.goal ? "F" : canal ? "" : arrow[snap.best[i]];
      return svg`&lt;g&gt;&lt;rect x="${pad + c * S}" y="${pad + r * S}" width="${S - 2}" height="${S - 2}"
          fill="${canal ? towData.style.water : shade(snap.value[i])}" stroke="#c3c2b7"/&gt;
        &lt;text x="${pad + c * S + S / 2 - 1}" y="${pad + r * S + S / 2 + 5}" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15"
          font-family="Helvetica" fill="${towData.style.ink}"&gt;${label}&lt;/text&gt;&lt;/g&gt;`;
    });
    const line = path.map(([r, c]) =&gt; `${pad + c * S + S / 2 - 1},${pad + r * S + S / 2 - 1}`).join(" ");
    return svg`&lt;svg width="${2 * pad + towData.cols * S}" height="${2 * pad + towData.rows * S + 22}" style="background:${towData.style.surface}"&gt;
      ${cells}&lt;polyline points="${line}" fill="none" stroke="${towData.style.blue}" stroke-width="3" opacity="0.8"/&gt;
      &lt;text x="${pad}" y="${2 * pad + towData.rows * S + 10}" font-size="13" font-family="Helvetica" fill="${towData.style.ink}"&gt;${name}: path of ${path.length - 1} steps&lt;/text&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;`;
  };
  return html`&lt;div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:16px"&gt;${panel("Q-learning")}${panel("Sarsa")}&lt;/div&gt;`;
}








Figure 2: Greedy actions and values on the towpath after the chosen number of training episodes (one run each; α = 0.5, ε = 0.1). Left: Q-learning. Right: Sarsa. The blue line is the greedy path; the light blue cells are the canal.






Learning rates, live
Draw a stream of bus waits (average 8 minutes) and compare a constant learning rate with a decaying one, \(\alpha = 1/m^p\).

viewof alphaConst = Inputs.range([0.01, 1], {step: 0.01, value: 0.1, label: "Constant α"})
viewof decayP = Inputs.range([0.3, 2], {step: 0.05, value: 1, label: "Decay exponent p"})
viewof waitSeed = Inputs.range([1, 50], {step: 1, value: 1, label: "Random stream"})























waitRun = {
  // Exponential waits with mean 8 from a seeded generator; running estimates from 0.
  const rand = d3.randomExponential.source(d3.randomLcg(waitSeed / 51))(1 / 8);
  let c = 0, d = 0;
  return d3.range(1, 201).map(m =&gt; {
    const x = rand();
    c += alphaConst * (x - c);
    d += Math.pow(m, -decayP) * (x - d);
    return {m, x, constant: c, decaying: d};
  });
}
md`After 200 waits: constant α gives **${waitRun[199].constant.toFixed(2)}**, decaying α gives **${waitRun[199].decaying.toFixed(2)}** (true mean 8).`
















Plot.plot({
  width: 600, height: 300, style: {background: "#fcfcfb"},
  x: {label: "wait number m"}, y: {label: "minutes", domain: [0, 25], grid: true},
  marks: [
    Plot.dot(waitRun, {x: "m", y: "x", r: 1.5, fill: "#898781", fillOpacity: 0.5, clip: true}),
    Plot.ruleY([8], {strokeDasharray: "3,3"}),
    Plot.line(waitRun, {x: "m", y: "constant", stroke: "#eb6834", strokeWidth: 2}),
    Plot.line(waitRun, {x: "m", y: "decaying", stroke: "#2a78d6", strokeWidth: 2})
  ]
})








Figure 3: Incremental estimates of the mean wait from one random stream of 200 waits: constant α (orange) and α = 1/m^p (blue), with the individual waits as grey dots and the true mean dotted.

Interactive

Bandit playground
Follow one run of each strategy on the headline test (click rates 0.35, 0.50, 0.55). The top chart shows each headline’s posterior after the chosen number of readers; the bottom shows each strategy’s regret so far, with the selected one highlighted.

play = FileAttachment("data/ch15-playground.json").json()
viewof strat = Inputs.select(play.names, {label: "Strategy", value: "Thompson sampling"})
viewof reader = Inputs.range([0, play.runs[play.names[0]].a.length], {step: 1, value: 20, label: "Readers so far"})























playNow = {
  // Replay the run: count clicks (wins) and non-clicks (losses) per headline, and the regret so far.
  const n = play.theta.length, best = Math.max(...play.theta);
  const regretOf = run =&gt; { let s = 0; return run.a.map(a =&gt; (s += best - play.theta[a])); };
  const run = play.runs[strat], w = Array(n).fill(0), l = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (let t = 0; t &lt; reader; t++) { if (run.r[t]) w[run.a[t]]++; else l[run.a[t]]++; }
  const regrets = play.names.map(name =&gt; ({name, values: regretOf(play.runs[name])}));
  return {w, l, regrets};
}
md`Shown so far: ${play.headlines.map((h, a) =&gt; `**${h}** ${playNow.w[a]} clicks / ${playNow.w[a] + playNow.l[a]} readers`).join(", ")}.`
















{
  // Beta(w + 1, l + 1) density with integer counts: log B(w+1, l+1) = log w! + log l! - log (w+l+1)!.
  const logFact = k =&gt; d3.sum(d3.range(1, k + 1), Math.log);
  const xs = d3.range(0.0025, 1, 0.005);
  const dens = play.headlines.flatMap((h, a) =&gt; {
    const w = playNow.w[a], l = playNow.l[a], lb = logFact(w) + logFact(l) - logFact(w + l + 1);
    return xs.map(x =&gt; ({h, x, y: Math.exp(w * Math.log(x) + l * Math.log(1 - x) - lb)}));
  });
  const top = Plot.plot({
    width: 600, height: 220, style: {background: play.style.surface, color: play.style.ink},
    x: {domain: [0, 1], label: "click rate θ"}, y: {label: "posterior density", grid: true},
    color: {domain: play.headlines, range: play.style.colors, legend: true},
    marks: [Plot.line(dens, {x: "x", y: "y", stroke: "h", strokeWidth: 2}),
            // Each true rate takes its headline's color through the color scale.
            Plot.ruleX(play.theta.map((t, a) =&gt; ({t, h: play.headlines[a]})), {x: "t", stroke: "h", strokeDasharray: "3,3"})]
  });
  const lines = playNow.regrets.flatMap(({name, values}) =&gt; values.map((v, t) =&gt; ({name, t: t + 1, v})));
  const bottom = Plot.plot({
    width: 600, height: 200, style: {background: play.style.surface, color: play.style.ink},
    x: {label: "readers"}, y: {label: "regret so far", grid: true},
    marks: [Plot.line(lines.filter(d =&gt; d.name !== strat), {x: "t", y: "v", z: "name", stroke: play.style.muted, strokeOpacity: 0.5}),
            Plot.line(lines.filter(d =&gt; d.name === strat), {x: "t", y: "v", stroke: play.style.ink, strokeWidth: 2.5}),
            Plot.ruleX([reader], {stroke: play.style.ink, strokeDasharray: "2,3"})]
  });
  return html`&lt;div&gt;${top}${bottom}&lt;/div&gt;`;
}








Figure 2: One run of the chosen strategy on the headline test. Top: each headline’s Beta posterior after the chosen number of readers, with the true click rates as dotted lines. Bottom: regret so far for all six strategies (the selected one in black).




This is one run, so luck matters: rerunning with another seed would reorder the strategies. Figure 5 averages over 2,000 random tests.


When should the optimal editor explore?
Headline B has the counts chosen below; headline A’s counts vary across the grid. Each cell shows whether the Bayes-optimal editor shows A or B next, for the chosen number of readers left. The dashed line marks where A’s and B’s posterior means are equal: greedy shows B only below and to the right of it, where A’s mean is lower.

optData = FileAttachment("data/ch15-optimal.json").json()
viewof bState = Inputs.select(optData.b_states, {label: "Headline B (clicks, no clicks)", format: d =&gt; `${d[0]} clicks, ${d[1]} no clicks`})
viewof left = Inputs.range([1, optData.r_max], {step: 1, value: 1, label: "Readers left"})























optCells = {
  const grid = optData.maps[`${bState[0]},${bState[1]}`][left - 1];
  return grid.flatMap((row, w) =&gt; row.map((b, l) =&gt; ({w, l, choice: b ? "B" : "A"})));
}
md`The optimal editor shows **B** in **${optCells.filter(d =&gt; d.choice === "B").length}** of ${optCells.length} cells; greedy would show B in **${optCells.filter(d =&gt; (d.w + 1) / (d.w + d.l + 2) &lt; (bState[0] + 1) / (bState[0] + bState[1] + 2)).length}**.`
















{
  // Cells as rectangles on linear axes (cell centers at integer counts), so the
  // equal-means line can be drawn in the same coordinates.
  const rb = (bState[0] + 1) / (bState[0] + bState[1] + 2);        // B's posterior mean
  // (w + 1)/(w + l + 2) = rb  &lt;=&gt;  w = (rb (l + 2) - 1) / (1 - rb)
  const line = d3.range(-0.5, optData.g - 0.5, 0.05).map(l =&gt; ({l, w: (rb * (l + 2) - 1) / (1 - rb)}))
    .filter(d =&gt; d.w &gt;= -0.5 && d.w &lt;= optData.g - 0.5);
  return Plot.plot({
    width: 420, height: 420, style: {background: optData.style.surface, color: optData.style.ink},
    x: {label: "A's non-clicks", domain: [-0.5, optData.g - 0.5]},
    y: {label: "A's clicks", domain: [-0.5, optData.g - 0.5]},
    color: {domain: ["A", "B"], range: [optData.style.orange, optData.style.blue], legend: true},
    marks: [
      Plot.rect(optCells, {x1: d =&gt; d.l - 0.47, x2: d =&gt; d.l + 0.47, y1: d =&gt; d.w - 0.47, y2: d =&gt; d.w + 0.47, fill: "choice"}),
      Plot.line(line, {x: "l", y: "w", stroke: optData.style.ink, strokeDasharray: "4,3"})
    ]
  });
}








Figure 3: The Bayes-optimal choice between headlines A and B, for A’s clicks (up) and non-clicks (across), given B’s counts and the readers left. The dashed line is where the two posterior means are equal.

Interactive

Choose λ for your critic
Pick the critic’s quality (from knowing nothing, \(\phi = 0\), to the least-squares fit) and \(\lambda\). Each dot is one gradient estimate from 10 rollouts, 300 in all. The star is the exact gradient. A poor critic with small \(\lambda\) gives tight but off-center clouds (bias); \(\lambda = 1\) gives centered but wide ones (variance).

gaeData = FileAttachment("data/ch13-gae.json").json()
viewof quality = Inputs.radio(new Map([["knows nothing (φ = 0)", 0], ["halfway", 0.5], ["fitted", 1]]),
  {label: "Critic", value: 0})
viewof lam = Inputs.radio(gaeData.lambdas, {label: "λ", value: 0})























gaeNow = {
  const key = `${quality === 1 ? "1.0" : quality === 0.5 ? "0.5" : "0.0"}|${lam === 0 ? "0.0" : lam === 1 ? "1.0" : lam}`;
  return {pts: gaeData.samples[key], st: gaeData.stats[key]};
}
md`Bias **${gaeNow.st.bias.toFixed(2)}**, spread (sd) **${gaeNow.st.sd.toFixed(2)}**, RMS error **${gaeNow.st.rms.toFixed(2)}**.`
















Plot.plot({
  width: 520, height: 400,
  style: {background: gaeData.style.surface, color: gaeData.style.ink},
